How to Create an Emergency Food Supply

Having an emergency food supply is an important part of being prepared for a disaster or other emergency events. An emergency food supply can provide you with a nutritiously balanced diet and help reduce stress when a potential event occurs.

In this article, we'll discuss the importance of having an emergency food supply and how to create one.

Identify Your Family's Needs

Creating an emergency food supply is an essential part of ensuring the safety and well- being of your family and pets in a time of crisis. The first step in developing a strategy for this purpose is to identify exactly what your family will need to survive any type of situation.

Think about how much food it will take to meet the nutritional needs of each family member while maintaining a reasonable level of energy. Consider any possible dietary restrictions that need to be accommodated, and make sure you include the whatever supplements, vitamins or medications are needed on a daily basis. Furthermore, make sure you have enough pet food or other supplies so your animal companions can remain healthy too!

It may be helpful to begin by categorizing all the items that should be included in your emergency supply kit:

  • Nonperishable items such as canned goods, dry cereal, granola bars and peanut butter;
  • Portable items like nuts, jerky and dried fruits;
  • Protein sources such as beans and lentils;
  • Milk products like powdered milk or shelf-stable milk;
  • Fruits like applesauce with no added sugars;
  • Vegetables such as carrots or celery sticks;
  • Cooking ingredients including salt, sugar and spices;
  • Water for drinking, cooking and cleaning.

Once you've identified all the components needed for emergency preparedness in terms of food supplies, you can create plans for where to store them and how long they will last before rotation becomes necessary.

Choose the Right Food Items

In order to create an emergency food supply for long-term storage, it's important to choose the right food items to stockpile. The basic staples of any long-term food supply are rice, beans, wheat, corn, oats and dehydrated foods such as dried fruit and instant soup mixes. These items can be purchased in large quantities at a fraction of the cost of store-bought canned goods.

